            {
              "section": "\\section{The Nature of Statistical Models}"
            },
            "\n",
            "\n",
            "\n",
            {
              "frame": [
                {
                  "begin": "\\begin{frame}"
                },
                [
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "frametitle": "\\frametitle{What is a Model?}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "centering": "\\centering"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "includegraphics": "\\includegraphics[width=.8\\textwidth]{figures/lego_1}"
                  },
                  " ",
                  "\\\\",
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "footnotesize": "\\footnotesize"
                  },
                  " Image: www.flickr.com/photos/brickset/ (CC BY\n  2.0) \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{This course is about statistical modeling.  So let's\n    start by asking, what is a model?}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Here's a model, built out of lego bricks.}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Some of you might have played with legos growing up, I definitely did.}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{The thing with legos is you learn some basic rules: the\n    ways that legos can fit together.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Then you can build something that resembles something in\n    the real world, that captures important behavior.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{When you're starting out with legos for the first time,\n    you don't immediately try to build a giant machine.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Maybe you take two legos, test them to see how they fit\n    together.  Once you're familiar with the pieces, you can then try\n    to create something that you see in the world. }"
                  },
                  " \n"
                ],
                {
                  "end": "\\end{frame}"
                }
              ]
            },
            "\n",
            "\n",
            {
              "frame": [
                {
                  "begin": "\\begin{frame}"
                },
                [
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "frametitle": "\\frametitle{What is a Statistical Model?}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "centering": "\\centering"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "includegraphics": "\\includegraphics[width=.8\\textwidth]{figures/modeling}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{A statistical model is also a representation of something in the world.}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{When you have data, it comes from a real-world system.}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{But we don't just care about this data, we have\n    questions: what data is coming next?  what other data could have\n    plausible come up? what features of the world are consistent with\n    this data?}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{For those questions, we can't interrogate the world\n    directly, we need a statistical model.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{This is a representation that also creates data.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{We have to pretend that this data actually came from the\n    model.  Then we can start to answer these questions.}"
                  },
                  " \n"
                ],
                {
                  "end": "\\end{frame}"
                }
              ]
            },
            "\n",
            "\n",
            {
              "frame": [
                {
                  "begin": "\\begin{frame}"
                },
                [
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "frametitle": "\\frametitle{Course Plan}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{When we designed this course, we decided to follow a\n    very strict narrative.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{At the highest level of abstraction, here's our plan...}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "enumerate": [
                      {
                        "begin": "\\begin{enumerate}"
                      },
                      [
                        "\n",
                        {
                          "item": "\\item"
                        },
                        {
                          "item": "\\item"
                        }
                      ],
                      {
                        "end": "\\end{enumerate}"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  "\n",
                  "\n",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Most courses jump around, looking at data, then building models, then back.}"
                  },
                  "\n",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{We believe it's better to really commit to understanding models, then apply them.}"
                  },
                  "\n",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Because of that, we're actually not going to touch any data for several weeks.}"
                  },
                  "\n",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{But don't worry, there will be a LOT of application in the second half of the course.}"
                  },
                  "\n",
                  "\n"
                ],
                {
                  "end": "\\end{frame}"
                }
              ]
            },
            "\n",
            "\n",
            "\n",
            {
              "frame": [
                {
                  "begin": "\\begin{frame}"
                },
                [
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "frametitle": "\\frametitle{First Models}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{When we start out, our models are not going to resemble the world at all}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{They will be brittle - with no flexibility to make them look like the real world}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Actually, just like this tower of legos, they may be very rectangle.}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{You may ask, where did this rectangular distribution come from?}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{The answer is we made it up!  and we just want to build\n    something simple to understand how the bricks fit together}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "centering": "\\centering"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "includegraphics": "\\includegraphics[width=.8\\textwidth]{figures/legos_3}"
                  },
                  " ",
                  "\\\\",
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "footnotesize": "\\footnotesize"
                  },
                  " Image: Hans Schou (CC BY-SA 3.0)\n"
                ],
                {
                  "end": "\\end{frame}"
                }
              ]
            },
            "\n",
            "\n",
            {
              "frame": [
                {
                  "begin": "\\begin{frame}"
                },
                [
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "frametitle": "\\frametitle{Useful Models}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "centering": "\\centering"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "includegraphics": "\\includegraphics[width=.4\\textwidth]{figures/disney_castle}"
                  },
                  " ",
                  "\\\\",
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "footnotesize": "\\footnotesize"
                  },
                  " Image: www.flickr.com/photos/brickset/ (CC BY 2.0)\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{But the key is not to worry about those problems at the\n    beginning.  as we learn more, we'll see that our models can be\n    more flexible, to represent a wide range of real world systems}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{We'll also learn how to fit our models to data, and\n    that's going to make them really useful.}"
                  },
                  " \n"
                ],
                {
                  "end": "\\end{frame}"
                }
              ]
            },
            "\n",
            "\n",
            "\n",
            {
