                        "\n  "
                      ],
                      {
                        "end": "\\end{columns}"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{How would you define probability?  Is it something\n    that you encounter in your day-to-day life?  A measure of\n    randomness in the world?}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Laplace was a famous mathematician, who developed what\n    we call the Bayesian interpretation of probability.  Let's read\n    what he said in 1814.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Laplace believed that, as humans, we are natural\n    processors of probability.  We think in probabilities, we use\n    probability when we speak to each other.}"
                  },
                  "   \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{So of course we think of probability as existing out in\n    the natural world.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{But, I'm here to tell you that probability is actually a\n    model.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{That's right, the randomness that we perceive around us\n    is a model that we use to make sense of the world. }"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Let me try to convince you with a few examples.}"
                  },
                  " \n",
                  "\n  ",
                  "% Others to consider:",
                  "\n  ",
                  "% ",
                  "\n  ",
                  "% \u201cFor every action, there's an infinity of outcomes. Countless trillions are possible, many milliards are likely, millions might be considered probable, several occur as possibilities to us as observers - and one comes true.\u201d",
                  "\n  ",
                  "% \u2015 China Mi\u00e9ville, The Scar",
                  "\n  ",
                  "% ",
                  "\n  ",
                  "% \u201cA likely impossibility is always preferable to an unconvincing possibility. The story should never be made up of improbable incidents; there should be nothing of the sort in it.\u201d",
                  "\n  ",
                  "% \u2015 Aristotle, Poetics",
                  "\n"
                ],
                {
                  "end": "\\end{frame}"
                }
              ]
            },
            "\n",
            "\n",
            "\n",
            {
              "frame": [
                {
                  "begin": "\\begin{frame}"
                },
                [
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "frametitle": "\\frametitle{Probability is A Model of the World}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "block": [
                      {
                        "begin": "\\begin{block}{Apocryphal quotations}"
                      },
                      [
                        "Apocryphal quotations",
                        "\n    ``",
                        {
                          "textit": "\\textit{All models are wrong; some are useful.}"
                        },
                        "'' ",
                        "\\\\",
                        "\n    ",
                        {
                          "hspace": "\\hspace{1em}"
                        },
                        " \u2014George Box\n  "
                      ],
                      {
                        "end": "\\end{block}"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  "\n",
                  "\n  Probability is:\n\n  ",
                  {
                    "itemize": [
                      {
                        "begin": "\\begin{itemize}"
                      },
                      [
                        "\n  ",
                        {
                          "item": "\\item"
                        },
                        {
                          "item": "\\item"
                        }
                      ],
                      {
                        "end": "\\end{itemize}"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  "\n",
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{But I'm here to tell you that probability is just a\n    model.  It's a very useful model - one that we can make\n    mathematically precise.  But it's a model of the world.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Let me try to convince you with a few examples.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{\\paul{Not sure how to fit the box quote into the\n      narrative - wonder if there's something more specific to\n      probability.}}"
                  },
                  " \n"
                ],
                {
                  "end": "\\end{frame}"
                }
              ]
            },
            "\n",
            "\n",
            {
              "frame": [
                {
                  "begin": "\\begin{frame}"
                },
                [
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "frametitle": "\\frametitle{Probability is a Model - Example 1}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "centering": "\\centering"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "includegraphics": "\\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/mil_doctors}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{First, say that you go to the doctor with some abdominal\n    pain.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{The doctor tells you that you have a 80\\% chance of\n    having kidney stones.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{But is there any randomness in whether you have kidney\n    stones?}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{If you walk out of the office and come back the next\n    day, will you have kidney stones 80\\% of the time?}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Of course not.  There's no randomness, you either have\n    kidney stones or you don't.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{The doctor is using probability in what we call it's\n    subjective sense -- as a strength of belief.}"
                  },
                  " \n"
                ],
                {
                  "end": "\\end{frame}"
                }
              ]
            },
            "\n",
            "\n",
            {
              "frame": [
                {
                  "begin": "\\begin{frame}"
                },
                [
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "frametitle": "\\frametitle{Probability is a Model - Example 2}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "centering": "\\centering"
                  },
                  " \n    ",
                  {
                    "includegraphics": "\\includegraphics[width=.8\\textwidth]{figures/forecast}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Next, you look at the weather forecast and you see that\n    there's a 50\\% chance of rain tomorrow.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Is there any randomness in whether it will rain?  It's a\n    future event, so you might think maybe.}"
                  },
                  " \n"
                ],
                {
                  "end": "\\end{frame}"
                }
              ]
            },
            "\n",
            "\n",
            {
              "frame": [
                {
                  "begin": "\\begin{frame}"
                },
                [
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "frametitle": "\\frametitle{Probability is a Model - Example 2 (cont.)}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "centering": "\\centering"
                  },
                  " \n    ",
                  {
                    "includegraphics": "\\includegraphics[width=.8\\textwidth]{figures/earth_copies}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{But what if you made 100 copies of the earth, and\n    arranges the atoms in each one perfectly in exactly the same\n    position with the same speed.  would it rain in your location in\n    half of them?}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Probably it would rain in all of them or none.  if we\n    could perfectly model how all the atoms in the atmosphere\n    interact, we'd know for sure whether it will rain.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Again, there's no real randomness - probability is just\n    a model for the world.}"
                  },
                  " \n"
                ],
                {
                  "end": "\\end{frame}"
                }
              ]
            },
            "\n",
            "\n",
            {
              "frame": [
                {
                  "begin": "\\begin{frame}"
                },
                [
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "frametitle": "\\frametitle{Probability is a Model - Example 3}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "centering": "\\centering"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "includegraphics": "\\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/coin}"
                  },
                  " ",
                  "\\\\",
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Finally, what about the most classic random event of all\n    - the coin flip.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Everyone knows that a coin lands heads 50\\% of the\n    time.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{But is that true?  What if you could flip the coin in\n    exactly the same way each time.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{And make sure every single atom in the air was moving in\n    exactly the same way.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{You would probably find that it lands on the same side\n    each time.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Really, the only people who work with true randomness\n    are quantum physicists.  The rest of us are using probability as a\n    model.}"
                  },
                  " \n"
                ],
                {
                  "end": "\\end{frame}"
                }
              ]
            },
            "\n",
            "\n",
            "\n",
            {
              "frame": [
                {
                  "begin": "\\begin{frame}"
                },
                [
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "frametitle": "\\frametitle{An Axiomatic Approach}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{We already have an intuitive model for probability in\n    our heads, but that's not enough to build statistical models - we\n    need a mathematically precise system.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{How do we create one?  We distill our intuition into a\n    small set of axioms.  This is what mathematicians do...}"
                  },
                  " \n  Why take an axiomatic approach?\n  ",
                  {
                    "itemize": [
                      {
                        "begin": "\\begin{itemize}"
                      },
                      [
                        "\n  ",
                        {
                          "item": "\\item"
                        },
                        {
                          "item": "\\item"
                        }
                      ],
                      {
                        "end": "\\end{itemize}"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  "\n",
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Suppose that you're rolling die -- you could observe that\n    the P(1|2) is just P(1) + P(2).  You could write that down as a fact.}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{\\paul{If you kept doing that, you'd end up with a huge\n      pile of facts, and you still would not be able to predict the\n      probability of a new event. You would have no logical way to\n      move from the one fact to another.}}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Instead, we need to identify axioms that explain the\n    probabilities of many different events. induct up to a higher set\n    of principles --  the pieces of the model that we need, that are\n    used to repredict to events.}"
                  },
                  "  \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{You might find this frustrating -- you might want to\n    study this like it is an observational science; counting lions or\n    cosmic rays. But, it isn't!}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Probability is a model, so we're inventing the rules,\n    not discovering them.  But if our system reminds us enough of the\n    real world, it can be useful.}"
                  },
                  "  \n  ",
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "textit": "\\textit{Thomas Kuhn's}"
                  },
                  " Philosophy of Science\n\n  ",
                  {
                    "itemize": [
                      {
                        "begin": "\\begin{itemize}"
                      },
                      [
                        "\n  ",
                        {
                          "item": "\\item"
                        },
                        {
                          "item": "\\item"
                        },
                        {
                          "item": "\\item"
                        }
                      ],
                      {
                        "end": "\\end{itemize}"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  "\n"
                ],
                {
                  "end": "\\end{frame}"
                }
              ]
