                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{The larger set is called a superset.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{We take every element that's in at least one of the\n    sets.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{We can write this: $A \\subseteq B$ if for every $x \\in\n    A, x \\in B$ }"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Most of the time, when we say subset, we mean that A is\n    inside B but A could be exactly the same as B.}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{If you ever really need to say that A is a subset and\n    actually smaller, you can add a slash through the bottom line.\n    that's called a strict subset or proper subset.}"
