                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{A die  -- $\\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\}$}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{A dartboard -- $\\{(x,y) \\text{ such that } x^{2} + y^{2} \\leq 1\\}$}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{A survey of 5 U.S. senators -- $\\{ S: S \\subseteq Senators, |S| = 5 \\}$ This is\n      not individual people, it's a set of sets! there are 100 choose 5 outcomes. }"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Suppose you're doing language translation --  $\\{ (w_1, w_2,...,w_k) : w_1,w_2,...w_k \\text{are words} \\} $.}"
                  },
                  "\n    ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Question: can you apply a probability measure directly to the set of outcomes?  unfortunately, no.  for simple ones you can, but what about the dartboard. there are an infinite number of locations, and the probability of each one is just zero.  but a function that's zero everywhere doesn't help us model this.}"
