            {
              "frame": [
                {
                  "begin": "\\begin{frame}"
                },
                [
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "frametitle": "\\frametitle{Event Spaces}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "block": [
                      {
                        "begin": "\\begin{block}{Intuition for an event space}"
                      },
                      [
                        "Intuition for an event space",
                        "\n    ",
                        {
                          "note": "\\note[item]{Now we have a bunch of events, and we want to group them\n      all together and call that an event space.}"
                        },
                        " \n    ",
                        {
                          "note": "\\note[item]{That seems like a simple idea, right?  But when you read\n      the textbook, you might have wondered, why is this so complicated?\n      well, it gets fairly technical, but it turns out that there are\n      sometimes sets of outcomes that we have to exclude from the event\n      space.  These are called non measurable.}"
                        },
                        " \n    ",
                        "\n    ",
                        {
                          "itemize": [
                            {
                              "begin": "\\begin{itemize}"
                            },
                            [
                              "\n    ",
                              {
                                "item": "\\item"
                              },
                              {
                                "item": "\\item"
                              }
                            ],
                            {
                              "end": "\\end{itemize}"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        "\n  "
                      ],
                      {
                        "end": "\\end{block}"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  "\n  ",
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{So Mathematicians said: we can't put all possible sets\n    in the event space, let's just make sure that any set we need for\n    a regular analysis is in there.  So they came up with a list of\n    guarantees and the technical term for these is a\n    $\\sigma$-algebra)}"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{You can think of this as making sure that any way you\n    can combine events together, the answer will always be an event,\n    so it will have a probability.  You can't accidentally fall out of\n    the event space. }"
                  },
                  " \n  ",
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "textbf": "\\textbf{Requirements for a $\\sigma$-algebra}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "itemize": [
                      {
                        "begin": "\\begin{itemize}"
                      },
                      [
                        "\n  ",
                        {
                          "item": "\\item"
                        },
                        {
                          "item": "\\item"
                        },
                        {
                          "item": "\\item"
                        }
                      ],
                      {
                        "end": "\\end{itemize}"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  "\n"
                ],
                {
                  "end": "\\end{frame}"
                }
              ]
            },
            "\n",
            "\n",
            "\n",
            "\n",
            "\n",
            {
              "frame": [
                {
                  "begin": "\\begin{frame}"
                },
                [
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Finally, the last element is the probability measure itself.  To be a valid probability measure, a function has to satisfy three properties.}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Where do these come from?  We're building a model here.  we're taking properties from our intuition and trying to write them down using math.}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{First, there are no negative probabilities.  that just makes sense to us, right?  what would a negative probability mean?}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Next, the probability of the entire outcome space is 1.  That should make sense too, because some outcome must happen. the state of the world will be something.}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{Finally, the addition rule.  Probabilities add up when we combine events, as long as they are disjoint.  The probability of rolling a 1 or a 2 is the probability of rolling a 1 plus the probability of rolling a 2.  But in standard probability theory that even works for a countably infinite set of disjoint events.}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "note": "\\note[item]{What's the probability that the sun goes supernova someday?  well, you can add the probability for today, plus the probability for tomorrow, and on forever.}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "frametitle": "\\frametitle{Probability Measure}"
                  },
                  "\n  ",
                  {
                    "block": [
                      {
                        "begin": "\\begin{block}{Definition of a probability measure}"
                      },
                      [
                        "Definition of a probability measure",
                        "\n    A function ",
                        {
                          "$": [
                            {
                              "begin": "$"
                            },
                            [
                              "P: S ",
                              {
                                "rightarrow": "\\rightarrow"
                              },
                              " ",
                              {
                                "mathbb": "\\mathbb{R}"
                              }
                            ],
                            {
                              "end": "$"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
